## 5. Translating the design into another application UI

The reference is a marketing website, but its visual language can be applied effectively to a creative studio, dashboard, canvas editor, or other functional web application.

The key is to reuse the design principles rather than reproduce the marketing page layout.

| Reference design element        | Translation into an application                                   |
| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Minimal top navigation          | Slim application header with logo, navigation, and primary action |
| White/gray alternating sections | Light gray application shell with white work surfaces             |
| Large product screenshots       | Main workspace or canvas remains the visual focus                 |
| Compact blue CTAs               | Primary actions such as Create, Generate, Save                    |
| Understated feature tabs        | Model, mode, workflow, or content selectors                       |
| Clean rounded cards             | Generation history, asset cards, templates, and settings panels   |
| Image-led bento grid            | Asset gallery, generation results, visual previews                |
| Small colored labels            | Model status, generation type, tags, or categories                |
| FAQ accordion styling           | Collapsible advanced settings and property groups                 |

For a canvas-based creative editor specifically, I would preserve a white or very light gray central working area, use compact neutral toolbars and inspectors, and reserve the blue accent for selection, active tools, and primary actions.

The colorful gradient treatment should be reserved for illustrations, thumbnails, preview frames, or occasional empty states—not every panel.

